{
  "id": "separation-amoebots",
  "model": "programmable-matter",
  "name": "Separation of coloured particles",
  "origin": {
    "ref": "Cannon2019Separation",
    "year": 2018,
    "note": "arXiv 2018; APPROX/RANDOM 2019."
  },
  "derived_from": {
    "problem": "compression-amoebots",
    "kind": "generalisation of",
    "relation": "Separation adds two colours to compression; the same local chain compresses and, depending on a second bias, separates or integrates the colours [Cannon2019Separation]."
  },
  "related": [
    {
      "problem": "separation",
      "relation": "Robots in the plane separate by hidden colours with deterministic algorithms; here a stochastic local rule separates two visible colours."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Particles of two colours, following a local stochastic rule, must cluster by colour (or mix).",
  "statement": "Each particle has one of two colours. Particles run a local Markov chain with a bias $\\lambda$ towards more neighbours and a bias $\\gamma$ towards neighbours of the same colour. The system should be compressed and **separated** (few edges between colours), or **integrated** (colours mixed), with high probability in the stationary distribution.",
  "primer": {
    "intro": "Think of a mixed crowd of red and blue marbles in a tray that, when shaken, sorts itself into a red patch and a blue patch, or stays mixed, depending on how 'sticky' same-coloured marbles are. Here $n$ particles on the triangular grid each carry a fixed colour, one of two; they start connected, have constant memory and no compass, and one randomly chosen particle acts at a time. A particle sits on one node of an endless triangular grid and moves in two steps: it *expands* into an empty neighbouring node, briefly occupying both, then *contracts* into one of the two; so one expansion and one contraction move it by one node. The group should become compressed (short perimeter, as in compression) and either *separated* (each colour mostly in one region, with few touching pairs of different colours) or *integrated* (colours mixed), whichever is asked for. As for compression, the question is what the group looks like after running for a long time, with high probability.",
    "ideas": [
      {
        "kind": "algorithm",
        "title": "The compression rule with a second bias for colour",
        "variant": "stochastic",
        "ref": "Cannon2019Separation",
        "idea": "There are two biases: $\\lambda$ rewards neighbours of any colour, and $\\gamma$ rewards neighbours of one's own colour. A randomly chosen particle does one of two things.\\n\\n1. **Step.** As in compression: pick a random empty neighbouring node; check locally that moving there neither splits the group nor encloses a hole; then move with probability $\\lambda^{\\text{(gain in neighbours)}}\\cdot\\gamma^{\\text{(gain in same-coloured neighbours)}}$ (certainly, if this number exceeds 1).\\n2. **Swap.** Pick a neighbour of the other colour and exchange places with it, with probability $\\gamma^{\\text{(gain in same-coloured neighbours of the two)}}$ (certainly, if above 1). Swaps let colours move through the middle of the group.",
        "analysis": "With only one colour the rule is exactly the compression rule [Cannon2016Compression]. For a strong colour bias, $\\gamma > 4^{5/4} \\approx 5.66$ together with $\\lambda\\gamma > 6.83$ (and $\\lambda > 1$), the group is compressed and separated with high probability [Cannon2019Separation]. For $\\gamma$ very close to 1, between $79/81$ and $81/79$, and $\\lambda$ large enough, it is compressed but integrated. So one rule does both jobs, and a single number set by the environment decides which.",
        "why_it_matters": "It showed that the biased-rule recipe extends from shape to internal organisation, here the arrangement of types."
      },
      {
        "kind": "lower-bound",
        "title": "A mild colour preference does not sort the colours",
        "variant": "stochastic",
        "ref": "Cannon2019Separation",
        "idea": "One might expect any preference $\\gamma > 1$ for same-coloured neighbours to sort the colours eventually. It does not. There are vastly more mixed arrangements of the colours inside a compressed blob than sorted ones: sorting means fixing a short border between the colours, while mixing leaves almost every particle free to be either colour. A slight preference gives each sorted arrangement only a slightly larger weight, which cannot make up for the huge number of mixed ones.",
        "analysis": "[Cannon2019Separation] prove that for $\\gamma$ between $79/81$ and $81/79$, which includes values slightly above 1, the group fails to separate with high probability: the number of touching pairs of different colours stays large. Separation needs the colour bias to beat this counting, which is why the positive result asks for $\\gamma > 5.66$. What happens for $\\gamma$ between these ranges is not settled by the paper.",
        "why_it_matters": "It is the colour version of the compression lesson: a preference must be strong enough to beat the number of disordered states."
      }
    ]
  },
  "history": "Separation extended the stochastic approach of compression to **heterogeneous** systems; the question is which bias parameters give which emergent behaviour [Cannon2019Separation].",
  "variants": [
